+
+## 2. Structural RCA — ci.yml job split (read at HEAD feda45e, `github_read_file`)
+
+The core CI defines exactly two jobs with **disjoint trigger `if` filters**:
+
+- `test` ("🧪 Core Pytest & Lint"):
+  `if: github.event_name == 'push' || 'pull_request' || (workflow_dispatch && inputs.run_autofix != true)`
+  → the product test suite **never executes on schedule runs**.
+- `autofix` ("🔧 PyErrorFix Engine"):
+  `if: github.event_name == 'schedule' || (workflow_dispatch && inputs.run_autofix == true)`
+  → the fixer engine runs **only on schedule or explicit manual dispatch**.
+
+Consequence: a scheduled nightly run contains **only** the PyErrorFix job. A red schedule run
+says **nothing** about product code health — the pytest/ruff/secret-scan plane did not run in it.
+The failure of the "PyErrorFix Engine" job is a failure of the autonomous-fixer ORCHESTRATION
+plane, not (so far provable) of product code. #2111's own honest-scope line ("NOT claimed:
+product-code breakage") is thereby structurally explained, not just unproven.
+
+## 3. Same-sha polarity — the decisive experiment GitHub already ran for us
+
+Run 35 (push, `419a838`) = **success**; run 36 (schedule, **same sha** `419a838`) = **failure**.
+Same tree, opposite verdicts, because the two runs executed **completely different job planes**
+(test-only vs autofix-only per §2). This falsifies any "bad commit 419a838" reading and any
+"product regression" reading in one stroke. The push-triggered product CI on core main has been
+**green on every push** in the parsed window (runs 35/37/38/39 all success).
+
+## 4. Failure-surface narrowing inside the autofix job (structural, no auth needed)
+
+Every pyerrorfix step is armored: the analyzer runs twice with shell-fallback semantics
+(its exit status is swallowed; SARIF output is redirected to a result file) and a
+post-step guard that **always rewrites a valid empty SARIF** if the output is missing/invalid.
+Therefore the analysis itself **cannot** hard-fail the job. Only three steps can:
+
+1. `actions/checkout@v5` with `fetch-depth: 0` (infra; rare);
+2. `actions/setup-python@v6` (infra; rare);
+3. `github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3` (`if: always()`) — **prime suspect**: on a private
+   repo, SARIF upload fails without GHAS/code-scanning entitlement ("Code scanning is not
+   enabled" class), an auth/entitlement failure independent of code content.
+
+Honest limit: the decisive error line is beyond the zero-credential log snippet window
+(independently re-confirmed this cycle — the snippet again covers setup/cleanup only, and shows
+the upload-sarif Node-20 deprecation warning, consistent with the job reaching that step).
+Maintainer's authenticated log view decides; the structural narrowing above tells them which
+~10 lines to read first. Note run 37's own title ("continue-on-error for SARIF upload") shows
+this exact plane has bitten core CI before and was patched once on the `test` job's path — the
+`autofix` job's upload step has no such tolerance.
+
+## 5. NEW latent defect found this RCA — `push.branches` filter corrupted at HEAD
+
+Current ci.yml at HEAD declares:
+
+```yaml
+on:
+  push:
+    branches: ain]
+```
+
+`ain]` is a literal YAML plain scalar — it matches **no branch** (clearly a corruption of
+`main`, the trailing `]` being an editing artifact). Inference chain that the bot's own commit
+introduced it: (a) push-triggered runs fired as late as run 39, 10-27T16:53:58Z, so the filter
+matched `main` at 832b27e; (b) the only commit landing on core main after 832b27e is the bot's
+`feda45e` (subject `ci`); (c) HEAD's filter is `ain]`; (d) zero push-triggered runs exist after
+run 39 in the full window while a schedule run did start on feda45e. **Net effect: product
+push-CI on core main has been silently dead since the bot's commit** — the nightly exercises
+only the fixer plane (§2), so core main's product code has had **no CI coverage at all** since
+10-27T16:53Z. This is strictly worse than the #2111 blind-spot finding: the runway is not
+merely unmonitored, its signal source is disconnected. (Caveat recorded honestly: (d) is
+window-bounded evidence, total_count=48; a maintainer commit-view of feda45e gives the
+one-keystroke confirmation.)
+
+## 6. Detection-plane audit — the watchdog path works; the residual gap is board visibility
+
+Both core jobs carry a `Trigger Federation CI Watchdog on Failure` step dispatching
+`federation_ci_failed` to `Supremeai-bd/supremeai-open` (`|| true`, PAT chain
+OPEN_SYNC_PAT/MERGE_PAT/OPEN_DISPATCH_TOKEN/GITHUB_TOKEN). It demonstrably fired for the 10-28
+failure: **P0 #2053's title names the very same run id 40653481018** (filed ~08:1xZ, claimed by
+contributor-agent 08:06:38Z, since CLOSED with its core-ci fix lane green). So the cross-repo
+detection lane #2111 calls a blind spot partially exists; the TRUE residual gap is that
+`bridge_work_queue` surfaces **open** items only — once #2053 closed, the recurring nightly
+failures (a second red the next night) raised no new board signal. #2111's sweep was the first
+re-detection ~3h later. Refinement, not contradiction, of the audit finding.
+
+## 7. Schedule-delay datum
+
+Cron is `0 2 * * *` (02:00Z, "Nightly PyErrorFix scan") but the schedule runs actually started
+07:23:29Z (10-27) and 07:58:01Z (10-28) — a ~5.4-6.0h GitHub scheduler queue delay both nights.
+Operational consequence: nightly-fixer output lands mid-morning UTC, and #2111's "the bot's own
+commit, pushed 07:58Z" reading is an artifact of that delay — the bot commit predates the run
+creation (a schedule run's head is simply the branch tip at delayed start time).
+
